  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Floodlit Court",
    "overview": "Dink Score has two lighting conditions. By day, the app is a calm, cool-grey phone UI: white soft-cornered cards on a pale slate ground, ink-dark type, nothing shouting. When a game starts, the scoring screen switches to a floodlit night court. It's a near-black radial field with two big rounded team panels, blue and green, lit from within. The numerals are so large and so squeezed that you can read them from the baseline. Tennis-ball yellow is the floodlight. It marks who is serving, what's live, and who's on top.\n\nThe surfaces are calm and tidy. Cards, tiles, chips and rows are restrained, and the numbers do the shouting. The one bold typographic idea runs through everything: the variable grotesque Bricolage Grotesque, squeezed to 75% width and set heavy, for every number that matters. That covers scores, ratings, ranks, totals and forecasts. Geist carries everything else at a comfortable reading size.\n\nIt must never read as a corporate fitness app (gradients everywhere, stock athletes, goal-crushing energy). Text never gets small or dense enough to need squinting outdoors.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Two worlds: a calm daylight app and a floodlit night court for scoring.",
      "Squeezed, heavy, tabular numerals for every number that matters.",
      "Ball yellow is a light source, used sparingly to mark serve, live and first place.",
      "Team identity is color: blue for team one, green for team two, carried from setup to scoring to history.",
      "Generous radii and big tap targets, so everything works one-handed and mid-game.",
      "Full light and dark themes, plus a forced theme via `data-theme`."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Floodlight Rule",
        "body": "Ball yellow means \"this one, now\": the server, the live game, the leader. It never becomes a general accent, a background wash or decoration. If two things on screen are yellow, one of them is wrong.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Team Color Rule",
        "body": "Blue is always team one and green is always team two, everywhere a team appears. Never swap them, and never use either one as plain decoration.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Squeezed Numeral Rule",
